<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  LOCAL FORK: the `com.apple.security.application-groups` entitlement was
	  `2BBY89MBSN.dev.warp`, which is upstream's team ID. An app group must be
	  prefixed with the team ID of the certificate signing it, and on Developer ID
	  it additionally needs an embedded provisioning profile that provisions the
	  group. Signing this fork under a different team with upstream's group is
	  simply wrong, and notarisation is entitled to reject it.

	  Dropping the key is behaviour-neutral here. `warp_core::paths::app_group_container_path`
	  probes the container by writing a temp file into it and returns `None` when
	  that fails, so `secure_state_dir()` already returns `None` without the
	  entitlement. All four callers fall back to `state_dir()`: the sqlite
	  database, the log manager, the profiler and the LSP logs. That fallback is
	  what every build of this fork has run on, since `Debug-Entitlements.plist`
	  never carried the key either.

	  Note for anyone tempted to re-add it under a new team: turning the app group
	  on moves the sqlite database out of `state_dir()` and into the group
	  container, so existing installs would come up with no history.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
